and justify your method.", "rubric": ["Measure mass and volume", "Calculate and compare densities from data"]}}}, {"depth": "applied", "title": "Lab or practical task", "block_type": "application", "sequence": 5, "required": true, "objective": "Use the concept in context", "content": {"body": "Use measurements or representations to investigate the scenario. State the conditions under which your method is appropriate.", "competencyKeys": ["applied-3"], "prerequisites": [], "task": {"prompt": "Plan a safe classroom comparison of two small, water-safe solid samples. Explain balance use, a suitable volume measurement, units, repeated readings and how you would compare the results.", "rubric": ["Plan a simple density investigation", "Record evidence and explain limitations"]}}}, {"depth": "applied", "title": "Reflection", "block_type": "synthesis", "sequence": 6, "required": true, "objective": "Use the concept in context", "content": {"body": "Reflect on your independent response and practical task. Identify the evidence supporting your conclusion and one change that would improve the method. Measure mass with a balance and volume with a suitable method. Divide thirty-six by twelve to obtain three grams per cubic centimetre. Compare materials using consistent units rather than mass alone.", "competencyKeys": [], "prerequisites": []}}, {"depth": "applied", "title": "Applied check", "block_type": "quick_check", "sequence": 7, "required": true, "objective": "Use the concept in context", "content": {"body": "Check your readiness to use evidence and explain a method. A workshop compares two solid materials. A sample has a mass of thirty-six grams and a volume of twelve cubic centimetres.", "competencyKeys": [], "prerequisites": [], "quickCheck": {"prompt": "Which response best supports a defensible conclusion in this scenario?", "options": ["Explain the method, use relevant evidence and state limitations", "Give a conclusion without connecting it to evidence"], "correctIndex"